<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Echigo Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Echigo Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast, corresponding to most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a>. In the 16th century it was the power base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle">Kasugayama Castle</a> and drew wealth from ramie cloth and mines. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> it was divided among several domains, including Nagaoka, and in 1868 these joined the northern alliance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/echigo-province.jpg" alt="Echigo Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Echigo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shogunate gave Nagaoka in <b>Echigo</b> to Makino Tadanari in 1618, with about 74,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagaoka-domain">Nagaoka Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Sendai and Yonezawa sought pardon for them, and when this was refused, delegates met at Shiroishi in May 1868 and formed an alliance, joined by Nagaoka and other <b>Echigo</b> domains.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nagao family were deputies of the Uesugi governors of <b>Echigo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
